            },
            {
              "ingredientSlug": "chicken-stock",
              "amount": 1.2,
              "unit": "litres",
              "prepNote": null,
              "isOptional": false,
              "groupLabel": null
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat the olive oil in a large saucepan over medium heat. Add the pancetta and cook for 5 minutes, stirring occasionally, until the fat has rendered and the edges are lightly crisp."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the onion, carrot, and celery. Cook gently for 10 minutes until soft and translucent. Add the garlic and rosemary sprigs and cook for 1 minute more."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Stir in the tomato puree and cook for 2 minutes. Add the borlotti beans and pour in the chicken stock. Bring to a gentle boil, then reduce to a simmer."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Remove the rosemary sprigs. Use a potato masher or the back of a spoon to roughly mash about a third of the beans directly in the pot. This thickens the soup without losing all the texture."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "If including pasta, stir in 160 g of ditalini or tubetti. Cook for the time stated on the packet, stirring often to prevent sticking. The soup will thicken further as the pasta cooks."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Taste and adjust seasoning. Ladle into bowls and finish with a drizzle of extra virgin olive oil and a few grinds of black pepper."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshootItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "The soup is too thin",
              "cause": "Not enough beans were mashed to release their starch.",
              "fix": "Mash another handful of beans directly in the pot and simmer for 5 more minutes. The soup will thicken quickly."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshootItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "The pasta has gone mushy",
              "cause": "The pasta was cooked too long or left sitting in the hot soup.",
              "fix": "Next time cook the pasta separately and add it to bowls at serving. This keeps it al dente."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshootItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "The soup tastes flat",
              "cause": "The soffritto was not cooked long enough to develop sweetness.",
              "fix": "Return to the heat and simmer for another 10 minutes. A splash of red wine vinegar at the end can brighten the flavour."
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Pasta e fagioli is eaten all across Italy. In the north it tends to be thick and creamy. In Naples it is often made with tomato and a richer broth. It is classic Friday night food, cooked at the start of the week and eaten through the cold days. Leftovers thicken overnight into something almost like a stew, which many cooks prefer."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
